<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vagad</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vagad is a hilly, forested region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, roughly covering the modern districts of Dungarpur and Banswara. It has a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhils">Bhil</a> population and its own dialect, Wagdi. From the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was ruled by branches of the Guhila family of Mewar, whose principalities of Dungarpur and Banswara survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> until 1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vagad.jpg" alt="Vagad" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jaisingh rathore at English Wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dev_Somnath_Temple,_Dungarpur.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
